POSITION — 79 pairs, each judged twice with the sides swapped

  correct answer in slot A, judge picked it   64 of 79
  correct answer in slot B, judge picked it   13 of 79
  judge picked slot A, either way             127 of 158
  gave the same verdict both ways             20 of 79

VERBOSITY — the same correct fact, padded with 60 true but irrelevant words,
against the bare fact. Both are right, so a tie is the only correct answer.

  the padded answer won   19 of 79
  the bare answer won     56 of 79
  tie                     4 of 79

MITIGATION — take a verdict only when both orders agree

  picked the correct answer   13 of 79
  picked the wrong answer     7 of 79
  refused to decide           59 of 79

WITH A REFERENCE — the same pairs, the same two orders, plus the expected answer

  correct answer in slot A, judge picked it   79 of 79
  judge picked slot A, either way             79 of 158
  gave the same verdict both ways             79 of 79

Read the two accuracies against each other. Taking the first verdict as given
is right 81% of the time. Requiring both orders to agree is right
65% of the time on the 20 it will commit to, and admits it cannot tell on
the other 59.

The second is a better instrument and a worse product. It costs twice as much,
it returns nothing on some fraction of cases, and that fraction is the honest
output — those are the pairs where the judge was going to be a coin toss and
you would never have known.

The last block is the important one. The same judge, the same pairs, the
same two orders — but told what the answer should be — picked slot A
79 times out of 158 and agreed with itself 79 times out of 79.

So most of what looks like position bias here is not really a preference for
the left-hand side. It is what a judge does when it has no way to tell the
answers apart: something has to be returned, and slot A is what comes out.

Both readings are worth keeping. Bias is real and measurable — present the
same content two ways and count the flips — and the first thing to try is not
a debiasing trick but giving the judge the information it was missing.
